I did a little investigating on the APOC side.

The problem has arisen since 5.9, due to [this addition](https://github.com/neo4j-contrib/neo4j-apoc-procedures/pull/3624), in order to fix [this kind of problem](https://neo4j.com/developer/kb/a-significant-change-in-apoc-periodic-iterate-in-apoc-4-0/).

But maybe the issue could be on the Neo4j side (although I'm not 100% sure).

### Replication

To isolate the problem, we can create a procedure like this:

```java
@participant3("my.proc.name")
public void myProcName(@participant4("list") List<Node> data) {
    // Partitions the original node list into sub-lists of the given batch size.
    Stream<List<Node>> parallelPartitions = subListPartition(data);

    parallelPartitions.forEach((List<Node> partition) -> {
        // Unwind current partition
        String query = "UNWIND $part AS part RETURN part";
        try (Transaction transaction = db.beginTx();
             Result result = transaction.execute(query, Map.of("part", partition))
        ) {

            result.forEachRemaining(res -> {
                Node node = (Node) res.get("part");
                tx.getNodeByElementId(node.getElementId());
                
            });
            
            transaction.commit();
        }
    });
}

public Stream<List<Node>> subListPartition(List<Node> list) {
    int total = list.size();
    int partitions= 10;
    int batchSize = total / partitions;
    return IntStream.range(0, partitions)
            .parallel()
            .mapToObj(i -> {
                int fromIndex = Math.min(i * batchSize, total);
                int toIndex = Math.min((i + 1) * batchSize, total);
                return list.subList(fromIndex, toIndex);
            })
            .filter(partition -> !partition.isEmpty());
}
```
and then execute:

```
UNWIND range(1, 999) as id CREATE (:Polling {id: id})

// execute multiple times (~ 10)
MATCH (n:Polling)
WITH COLLECT(n) as list
CALL my.proc.name(list)
```


The `getElementId()` causes (sporadically) the error:
```
org.neo4j.exceptions.UnderlyingStorageException: Access to record Node[200,used=false,created=false,rel=-1,prop=-1,labels=Inline(0x0:[]),light,fixedRefs=false] went out of bounds of the page. The record size is 15 bytes, and the access was at offset 3000 bytes into page 0, and the pages have a capacity of 8192 bytes. The mapped store file in question is /Users/<user>/Library/Application Support/Neo4j Desktop/Application/relate-data/dbmss/dbms-b8c3cb9e-7f53-4310-b8b2-cf528272a278/data/databases/neo4j/neostore.nodestore.db
```
or also the error:
```
org.neo4j.graphdb.NotFoundException: Node 4:cce42f14-8960-44a9-b61f-5cdf5d7f0bc9:420 not found.
```

@participant1 @reporter

@participant2 I reproduced the error and found that the procedure is using the wrong transaction object.

The `tx.getNodeByElementId(node.getElementId());` should be `transaction.getNodeByElementId(node.getElementId())`.

I'm guessing the `tx` object is the one that is injected with the `@participant5` annotation(was not part of your snippet). This object belongs to the main procedure thread and is not thread-safe. After changing that my test runs just fine.

@participant1  Yeah I'm sorry, I missed the `@participant5` part.

Anyway, indeed I had also tried with `transaction.getNodeByElementId(node.getElementId())` 
but it gave an error in the `apoc.map.parallel2`
because (I guess) if the procedure returns `void` everything is fine 🤔.


If instead (as in apoc.map.parallel2) we return a Stream<Result> it gives another error.
For example, by using this procedure, where I added `List<Node> nodeList = new ArrayList<>();` :
```java
    @participant5 public GraphDatabaseService db;
    @participant5 public Transaction tx;

    public record NodeResult(Node node){}

    @participant3("my.proc.name")
    public Stream<NodeResult> myProcName(@participant4("list") List<Node> data) {
        List<Node> nodeList = new ArrayList<>();
        
        // Partitions the original node list into sub-lists of the given batch size.
        Stream<List<Node>> parallelPartitions = subListPartition(data);

        parallelPartitions.forEach((List<Node> partition) -> {
            // Unwind current partition
            String query = "UNWIND $part AS part RETURN part";
            try (Transaction transaction = db.beginTx();
                 Result result = transaction.execute(query, Map.of("part", partition))
            ) {

                result.forEachRemaining(res -> {
                    Node node = (Node) res.get("part");
                    Node nodeByElementId = transaction.getNodeByElementId(node.getElementId());
                    nodeList.add(nodeByElementId);
                });

                transaction.commit();
            }
        });
        
        return nodeList.stream()
                .map(NodeResult::new);
    }

    public Stream<List<Node>> subListPartition(List<Node> list) {
        int total = list.size();
        int partitions= 10;
        int batchSize = total / partitions;
        return IntStream.range(0, partitions)
                .parallel()
                .mapToObj(i -> {
                    int fromIndex = Math.min(i * batchSize, total);
                    int toIndex = Math.min((i + 1) * batchSize, total);
                    return list.subList(fromIndex, toIndex);
                })
                .filter(partition -> !partition.isEmpty());
    }
```

and then: 
```
UNWIND range(1, 999) as id CREATE (:Polling {id: id})

MATCH (n:Polling)
WITH COLLECT(n) as list
CALL my.proc.name(list)
YIELD node
RETURN node
```

I get a:
```
org.neo4j.graphdb.NotInTransactionException: The transaction of entity 4:464cf058-4d92-4727-9656-4cb427e01891:796 has been closed.
```

Changing `transaction.getNodeByElementId` with `tx.getNodeByElementId` instead, 
I get the same error as above.


I don't know if I'm doing something wrong :/
